<DocsLayout title="Architecture" activeSlug="architecture" description="How PAS-Agent is built: a Rust core library and CLI, a single JSON session file, and Markdown exports agents read at startup.">
  <h1>Architecture</h1>

  <p>
    PAS-Agent is a minimal, local-first tool: one small Rust binary and one JSON file per
    project. This page explains how the pieces fit together.
  </p>

  <h2>How It Works</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>One file holds the session.</strong> <code>init</code> creates
      <code>.pas-agent/session.json</code> at the root of your git repository (it asks
      <code>git rev-parse --show-toplevel</code> where that is). Every other command walks up from
      the current directory until it finds that file, so they work from any subfolder.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Commands edit the file.</strong> <code>update</code> and <code>remove</code> load the
      session, change the task state, and save it again. Saving writes a temporary file and renames
      it over the old one, so a crash or a full disk can't leave a half-written session.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Checkpoints ask git what changed.</strong> <code>checkpoint</code> runs
      <code>git</code> for the branch, the commit, and <code>git status</code>, then stores that
      next to a copy of the task state. PAS-Agent's own directory is excluded, so the session never
      shows up as "changed".
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Export renders Markdown.</strong> <code>export</code> refreshes the git state and turns
      the session into Markdown. Every value is flattened to a single line and escaped, so nothing you
      type can break the document or fake its markers.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The block goes where the agent looks.</strong> The Markdown is wrapped in
      <code>pas-agent:start</code> / <code>pas-agent:end</code> markers and written into the file the
      target agent loads at startup. If the file has a block, only the block is replaced; if not, the
      block is appended. Your own content is never touched.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The next agent just reads a file.</strong> No plugin or API is involved. The agent loads
      its instruction file as usual and finds the task, progress, and decisions in it.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    The session format carries a <code>schema_version</code>. New fields get defaults, so older files
    keep loading, and a file from a newer version is rejected rather than misread. PAS-Agent makes no
    network requests at any point.
  </p>

  <h2>System Overview</h2>
  <pre><code>PAS-Agent CLI
    ↓ writes
.pas-agent/session.json  (portable state)
    ↓ generates
CLAUDE.md / AGENTS.md / GEMINI.md  (inside a marked block)
    ↓ read at startup by
Claude Code / Codex / OpenCode / Cursor / Kiro CLI / Antigravity / Gemini CLI</code></pre>

  <h2>Crate Structure</h2>
  <pre><code>crates/
├── pas-agent-core/     # Session model, storage, context generation
└── pas-agent-cli/      # CLI binary (clap-based)</code></pre>

  <h3>pas-agent-core</h3>
  <p>
    Core library with no CLI dependencies. Handles:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Session data model (<code>Session</code>, <code>TaskState</code>, <code>Checkpoint</code>, <code>FileState</code>, <code>GitRef</code>, <code>Agent</code>)</li>
    <li>File-based session storage (<code>SessionStore</code>)</li>
    <li>Context generation and non-destructive writing (<code>generate_context</code>, <code>write_context_file</code>)</li>
    <li>Git state capture (<code>capture_git_state</code>, <code>get_changed_files</code>)</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>pas-agent-cli</h3>
  <p>
    CLI binary providing user-facing commands: <code>init</code>,
    <code>checkpoint</code>, <code>status</code>, <code>list</code>,
    <code>export</code>, <code>update</code>, <code>remove</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>Storage Format</h2>
  <p>
    One versioned JSON file per project, written atomically. No database is required.
  </p>
  <pre><code>.pas-agent/
└── session.json   # task state, git state, checkpoints</code></pre>

  <h2>Integration Model</h2>
  <p>
    PAS-Agent does not parse internal agent session formats. Instead, it
    generates instruction files that each agent reads at session start:
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Agent</th>
        <th>File</th>
        <th>Reads At</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>Claude Code</td>
        <td><code>CLAUDE.md</code></td>
        <td>Session start</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Codex</td>
        <td><code>AGENTS.md</code></td>
        <td>Session start</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>OpenCode</td>
        <td><code>AGENTS.md</code></td>
        <td>Session start</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Kiro CLI</td>
        <td><code>AGENTS.md</code></td>
        <td>Session start</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Antigravity</td>
        <td><code>AGENTS.md</code></td>
        <td>Session start</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Cursor</td>
        <td><code>AGENTS.md</code></td>
        <td>Session start</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Gemini CLI</td>
        <td><code>GEMINI.md</code></td>
        <td>Session start</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Design Decisions</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Decision</th>
        <th>Rationale</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>JSON for storage</td>
        <td>Simple, human-readable, no database dependency</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Flat <code>.pas-agent/</code> directory</td>
        <td>No hidden config complexity, easy to inspect</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Marked export block</td>
        <td>Coexists with hand-written instruction files instead of overwriting them</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>schema_version</code> + field defaults</td>
        <td>Older session files keep loading; newer ones fail loudly instead of being misread</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Markdown export</td>
        <td>All target agents read markdown instruction files at session start</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>No daemon</td>
        <td>Nothing runs in the background; every change happens when you run a command</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>No AI in core</td>
        <td>Deterministic and offline; any future AI-assisted features stay optional</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</DocsLayout>
